{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01RPqpVW6WN8BBQ5svN9WWSP", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cat_and_mouse\"}, {\"url\": \"https://jann.wordreference.com/enko/draw\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.etymonline.com/es/word/rat-catcher\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.kyeonggi.com/article/201903261051530\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.fnnews.com/news/202606291509347302\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.etymonline.com/kr/word/rat-catcher\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.etymonline.com/kr/word/rat-trap\"}, {\"url\": \"https://pressian.com/pages/articles/102432\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.etymonline.com/kr/word/rat-terrier\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:54:20.641Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "\"소 뒷걸음질 치다 쥐 잡기\" 뜻 우연히", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01RBEfZaR5FUP9cDq6VxGKfN", "content": [{"title": "Cat and mouse", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cat_and_mouse"}, {"title": "draw back", "url": "https://jann.wordreference.com/enko/draw back"}, {"title": "rat catcher", "url": "https://www.etymonline.com/es/word/rat-catcher"}, {"title": "www.kyeonggi.com", "url": "https://www.kyeonggi.com/article/201903261051530"}, {"title": "www.fnnews.com", "url": "https://www.fnnews.com/news/202606291509347302"}, {"title": "rat catcher", "url": "https://www.etymonline.com/kr/word/rat-catcher"}, {"title": "rat trap", "url": "https://www.etymonline.com/kr/word/rat-trap"}, {"title": "pressian.com", "url": "https://pressian.com/pages/articles/102432"}, {"title": "rat terrier", "url": "https://www.etymonline.com/kr/word/rat-terrier"}]}, "The search didn't turn up any source that explains this idiom. The results were about English idioms like \"cat and mouse,\" rat-catching history in Korea, and word etymologies, so none of them I could cite defines 소 뒷걸음질 치다 쥐 잡기.\n\nFrom general knowledge, the idiom means achieving a result by accident or luck rather than through skill or effort. The image is an ox that stumbles backward and happens to catch a mouse. It's used when someone succeeds without really trying or planning, or when a good outcome comes out of an unintended action. A search for a Korean dictionary entry, such as the Standard Korean Dictionary (표준국어대사전), would confirm the exact wording and usage."], "durationSeconds": 4.7735841249999975, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01RPqpVW6WN8BBQ5svN9WWSP", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
